  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:2.35pt;margin-b=
ottom:
  0in;margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:=
12.0pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial'>An
  algorithm is a clear set of ordered steps used to solve a problem. The
  important word is ordered. A random pile of advice is not an algorithm. A
  picture of the result is not an algorithm. A vague goal like &quot;make
  pizza&quot; is not an algorithm. The algorithm is the specific path from =
the
  starting point to the finished result.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
 <tr style=3D'mso-yfti-irow:2;height:.25in'>
  <td width=3D704 valign=3Dtop style=3D'width:528.0pt;border:none;border-bo=
ttom:solid #F667A8 1.0pt;
  mso-border-bottom-alt:solid #F667A8 .75pt;background:#F667A8;padding:4.9p=
t 5.75pt 0in 7.0pt;
  height:.25in'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:0in'><b style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-weight:
  normal'><span style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-he=
ight:
  107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;color:w=
hite'>Full
  Teaching Explanation</span></b></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
 <tr style=3D'mso-yfti-irow:3;mso-yfti-lastrow:yes;height:237.4pt'>
  <td width=3D704 style=3D'width:528.0pt;border:solid #F667A8 1.0pt;border-=
top:
  none;mso-border-top-alt:solid #F667A8 .75pt;mso-border-alt:solid #F667A8 =
.75pt;
  background:#FDE6F0;padding:4.9pt 5.75pt 0in 7.0pt;height:237.4pt'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:2.55pt;margin-b=
ottom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>This lesson introduces algorithms in the
  friendliest way possible: by showing that you already use them constantly.
  When you order pizza for friends, you do not simply shout &quot;pizza&quo=
t;
  into the universe and hope dinner materializes. You follow a sequence: co=
unt
  how many people are eating, ask what toppings they want, choose the size =
and
  number of pizzas, call or order from the store, and pay.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:.4pt;margin-bot=
tom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>That ordered list matters because each step
  depends on earlier information. You cannot choose the right number of piz=
zas
  before you know how many people are eating. You cannot order accurately
  before you know toppings. You cannot pay before the order exists. The les=
son
  is training you to see that solving problems is often about arranging the
  work in the right order.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:1.45pt;margin-b=
ottom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>In programming, an algorithm is the plan
  behind the code. Python syntax is how you express the plan, but the algor=
ithm
  is the thinking underneath it. Two students can write different Python co=
de
  and still use the same algorithm. They may name variables differently or
  format the code differently, but if the steps and decisions are the same,=
 the
  algorithm is basically the same.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:.95pt;margin-bo=
ttom:
  0in;margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:=
12.0pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial'>The
  key beginner mistake is thinking algorithms are automatically complicated.
  They are not. A simple recipe, a checkout process, or a set of directions=
 can
  be an algorithm. Later, algorithms can become much more formal, but the
  foundation is still the same: a goal, an ordered process, and a way to kn=
ow
  when the task is finished.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
