  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:5.05pt;margin-b=
ottom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>This lesson pushes the algorithm idea beyo=
nd
  pizza and into normal daily routines. Imagine trying to teach a robot how=
 to
  brush its teeth. You cannot say, &quot;just brush your teeth,&quot; becau=
se
  the robot has no hidden common sense. You <span class=3DGramE>have to</sp=
an>
  describe the task in steps: go to the sink, pick up the toothbrush, put
  toothpaste on the brush, turn on the water, brush, rinse, and put everyth=
ing
  back.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:.05in;margin-bo=
ttom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>That example is funny because humans skip
  these details automatically. We use background knowledge. We understand t=
hat
  toothpaste belongs on the brush and that the brush goes in your mouth, no=
t in
  your ear. Computers do not get that luxury. In programming, every importa=
nt
  action must be represented somehow. Algorithms are how we make invisible
  human knowledge visible.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:6.65pt;margin-b=
ottom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>The lesson also uses examples like washing
  clothes, giving a weekly injection, and checking a bank balance on your
  phone. Each task has a starting point, a goal, and a repeatable process. =
If
  you do the steps in a different order, the result may be wrong or unsafe.=
 For
  example, with laundry, you do not start the dryer before the clothes have
  been washed. With checking <span class=3DGramE>a bank</span> balance, you=
 do
  not check the balance before unlocking the phone and opening the app.</sp=
an></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial'>This
  is why algorithms help programming. Programs are built out of procedures.=
 A
  login process, checkout flow, search feature, photo upload, bank transfer,
  medication reminder, or order tracker all require a sequence of precise
  actions. When you learn to describe real-life tasks algorithmically, you =
are
  learning to design code before you type code.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
