  background:#EDE8FF;padding:4.9pt 5.75pt 0in 7.0pt;height:40.8pt'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial'>This
  lesson turns real-life tasks into algorithmic thinking by focusing on one
  clean rule: one action per line. When steps are too broad or smashed
  together, the algorithm becomes harder to follow and harder to translate =
into
  code.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
 <tr style=3D'mso-yfti-irow:2;height:.25in'>
  <td width=3D704 valign=3Dtop style=3D'width:528.0pt;border:none;border-bo=
ttom:solid #F667A8 1.0pt;
  mso-border-bottom-alt:solid #F667A8 .75pt;background:#F667A8;padding:4.9p=
t 5.75pt 0in 7.0pt;
  height:.25in'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:0in'><b style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-weight:
  normal'><span style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-he=
ight:
  107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;color:w=
hite'>Full
  Teaching Explanation</span></b></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
 <tr style=3D'mso-yfti-irow:3;mso-yfti-lastrow:yes;height:225.1pt'>
  <td width=3D704 style=3D'width:528.0pt;border:solid #F667A8 1.0pt;border-=
top:
  none;mso-border-top-alt:solid #F667A8 .75pt;mso-border-alt:solid #F667A8 =
.75pt;
  background:#FDE6F0;padding:4.9pt 5.75pt 0in 7.0pt;height:225.1pt'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:6.65pt;margin-b=
ottom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>Lesson 3 takes the idea of algorithms and
  makes it more practical. It asks you to take an everyday task, like making
  hot chocolate, making a sandwich, or sending a text message, and <span
  class=3DGramE>break</span> it into clear, ordered instructions. This is t=
he
  same skill you will use when turning a programming problem into pseudocod=
e.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:5.7pt;margin-bo=
ttom:
  12.3pt;margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>The hot chocolate example starts with a va=
gue
  goal: make hot chocolate. A better algorithm begins with concrete steps: =
get
  a mug, pour milk into the mug, heat the milk, add chocolate, stir, and se=
rve.
  The sandwich example gets even more detailed. Instead of saying &quot;mak=
e a
  sandwich,&quot; the algorithm says to get a plate, get bread, gather
  ingredients, put two slices of bread on the plate, apply condiments, add
  fillings, put the bread together, and eat.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:12.3pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:115%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial'>The important idea is that each line should do one small thing. If=
 a
  line <span class=3DGramE>says</span> &quot;get condiments, get meat, get
  cheese, and assemble the sandwich,&quot; it is doing too much. A programm=
er
  reading that line <span class=3DGramE>has to</span> mentally unpack it. A
  computer would need those actions separated anyway. Good algorithms reduce
  that unpacking work by making the steps visible.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial'>This
  is also where pseudocode becomes useful. Pseudocode is not about perfect
  Python syntax. It is about making the plan readable. Before you write a l=
oop,
  condition, function, or data structure, you can sketch the behavior in pl=
ain
  English. That sketch helps you see missing steps, bad order, repeated wor=
k,
  and edge cases before the code gets involved and starts flinging syntax
  errors like confetti.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
</table>

