om:6.0pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:112%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:112%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial;color:#161616'>The point of this lesson is to strengthen your algor=
ithm
  instinct before the course gets more technical. The screenshots walk thro=
ugh
  everyday examples like laundry, making a sandwich, sending a text, boiling
  water for tea, brushing your teeth, and helping a friend make a peanut bu=
tter
  and jelly sandwich. Those examples may look <span class=3DGramE>simple</s=
pan>,
  but they train the same thinking used in code: break the task into ordered
  instructions that a person or computer could follow.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:6.0pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:112%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:112%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial;color:#161616'>A strong algorithm has three important qualities. Fi=
rst,
  it is ordered. If the steps happen in the wrong order, the result can fai=
l.
  You cannot press send before typing the message, and you cannot start the
  washing machine before putting clothes and soap inside. Second, each step=
 is
  actionable. A good step tells the doer what to do, not just what the final
  goal is. Third, the steps are clear enough that someone else could follow
  them without guessing.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial;
  color:#161616'>This is why the lesson keeps pushing you toward pseudocode
  style: numbered steps, verb-first instructions, and one clear action per
  line. Pseudocode is not Python yet. It is a planning language that lets y=
ou
  prove the logic makes sense before you worry about exact syntax.</span></=
p>
  </td>
 </tr>
