  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:6.0pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:112%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:112%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial;color:#161616'>The lesson starts with a postcard or stamping exampl=
e:
  if you have 3 postcards and do 2 stamping moves for each postcard, the to=
tal
  work grows as the number of postcards grows. Then it moves into code with=
 a
  list of names. The computer goes through the list one name at a time. Each
  loop pass is one unit of work for the repeated part of the algorithm.</sp=
an></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:.55pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial;
  color:#161616'>This is a major bridge between normal coding and algorithm
  analysis. Beginners often count only the lines of code they see.</span></=
p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:6.0pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:112%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:112%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial;color:#161616'>But computers do not run a loop line just once. A <s=
pan
  class=3DGramE>loop</span> body runs once per item. If the list has 3 name=
s, the
  body runs 3 times. If the list has 6 names, the same body runs 6 times. T=
he
  code did not get longer, but the amount of work changed because the input=
 got
  bigger.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:112%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:112%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial;color:#161616'>That idea matters because real programs rarely proce=
ss
  only 3 cute example items. They process photos, contacts, orders, message=
s,
  users, records, or search results. A tiny loop can be harmless with a sma=
ll
  input and slow with a huge input.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial;
  color:#161616'>Counting work helps you predict how an algorithm will beha=
ve
  before it becomes a problem.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
