di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:113%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial'>Lesson 8 turns Big O from vocabulary into a reading skill. Instead=
 of
  only recognizing the labels <span class=3DGramE>O(</span>1), O(n), and O(=
n^2),
  you practice looking at code and deciding which growth pattern it matches=
.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:8.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial'>The
  main move is to focus on the parts of the code that grow with the input.
  Fixed setup lines, fixed print statements, and constant multipliers matte=
r in
  real programs, but they do not usually change the Big O class. Big O keeps
  the fastest-growing part because that is the part that dominates when n g=
ets
  large.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
 <tr style=3D'mso-yfti-irow:2;height:21.0pt'>
  <td width=3D710 valign=3Dtop style=3D'width:7.4in;border:none;border-bott=
om:solid #F45D9A 1.0pt;
