  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:0in'><b style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-weight:
  normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-fa=
mily:
  Arial;color:white'>Full Teaching Explanation</span></b></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
 <tr style=3D'mso-yfti-irow:3;mso-yfti-lastrow:yes;height:124.8pt'>
  <td width=3D710 style=3D'width:7.4in;border:solid #F45D9A 1.0pt;border-to=
p:none;
  mso-border-top-alt:solid #F45D9A .75pt;mso-border-alt:solid #F45D9A .75pt;
  background:#FDE6EF;padding:6.5pt 5.75pt 0in 6.0pt;height:124.8pt'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:6.0pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:113%'><span style=3D'font-size:8.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:113%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial'>When you analyze code, do not start by counting every tiny operati=
on
  forever. Start by identifying the shape. A plain statement like <b
  style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>total =3D 0</b> is <span class=3DGr=
amE>O(</span>1)
  because it runs once. A loop over a list is usually O(n) because it runs =
once
  for each item. A loop inside another loop is often O(n^2) because the inn=
er
  loop repeats for every pass of the outer loop.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:6.0pt;
  margin-left:1.0pt;line-height:113%'><span style=3D'font-size:8.5pt;mso-bi=
di-font-size:
  12.0pt;line-height:113%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial'>The course examples use simple step counts to build intuition. If a
  list has 3 names, a loop runs 3 times. If the list grows to 6 names, the =
loop
  runs 6 times. That is linear growth. But if each item <span class=3DGramE=
>has
  to</span> be compared with every other item, the work grows much faster
  because every new item creates many new pair checks.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:1.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:8.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial'>Big
  O also drops details that do not affect the long-term shape. In 5n + 7, t=
he
  +7 is fixed extra work and the 5 is a constant multiplier, so the express=
ion
  becomes O(n). In n^2 + 5n + 20, the n^2 term grows faster than the linear=
 and
  constant parts, so the whole expression becomes O(n^2). This is the
  &quot;dominant term&quot; rule.</span></p>
