  background:#FFE6F0;padding:8.2pt 5.75pt 0in 8.0pt;height:238.4pt'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:26.4pt;line-height:108%'><span
  style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:108%;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>Algorithm
  analysis is the skill of looking beneath the surface of code. Two programs
  can both be correct, but one may stay fast as the list grows while the ot=
her
  becomes painfully slow. In this lesson, you practice recognizing the comm=
on
  shapes: fixed work, one <span class=3DGramE>pass</span> through a list, n=
ested
  passes, and expressions where one term eventually dominates the others.</=
span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:26.4pt;line-height:108%'><span
  style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:108%;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>The most
  important habit is to identify what depends on the input size. If a line
  always runs once, it is constant work. If a loop runs once for every item=
 in
  the list, the work grows with the list, so it is linear. If one loop runs
  inside another and both depend on the same input, the work usually grows =
like
  pairs of items, which is quadratic. If you see an expression like n^2 + 1=
2n,
  the n^2 part eventually dominates, so the simplified Big O is O(n^2).</sp=
an></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:0in'><span style=3D'font-size=
:10.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>The challenge questions also teach you to
  avoid over-counting. Big O is not asking for the exact number of steps. I=
t is
  asking for the growth shape. That means <span class=3DGramE>constants</sp=
an>
  and smaller terms get dropped after you understand what they mean. This d=
oes
  not mean constants never matter in real apps; it means Big O is zoomed out
  enough to compare how algorithms scale.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
