  </td>
 </tr>
 <tr style=3D'mso-yfti-irow:3;height:225.2pt'>
  <td width=3D710 style=3D'width:7.4in;border:solid #F45B9A 1.0pt;border-to=
p:none;
  mso-border-top-alt:solid #F45B9A .75pt;mso-border-alt:solid #F45B9A .75pt;
  background:#FFE6F0;padding:8.2pt 5.75pt 0in 8.0pt;height:225.2pt'>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:26.4pt;line-height:108%'><span
  style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:108%;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>The lesson
  compares solutions to the same task so you can see the trade-off between
  runtime and readability. One version may loop through a list once and kee=
p a
  running total. Another version may use nested loops or unnecessary extra
  work. Both might eventually calculate the correct answer, but their growth
  patterns are different.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:26.4pt;line-height:108%'><span
  style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:108%;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>For small
  input, the difference may not feel dramatic. If a list has three items, O=
(n)
  and O(n^2) may both finish instantly. The difference appears when the inp=
ut
  grows. With 1,000 items, an O(n) solution might do about 1,000 pieces of
  work, while an O(n^2) solution might do about 1,000,000 pair checks. That=
 is
  why Big O matters before the code becomes painfully slow.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:0in'><span style=3D'font-size=
:10.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>This lesson also adds nuance: faster is not
  the only measurement. If two solutions have the same Big O, the clearer o=
ne
  is usually better. Readability matters because future-you, classmates,
  teammates, and instructors need to understand the logic. A clever trick t=
hat
  technically works but nobody can explain is often worse than a
  straightforward solution with the same growth class.</span></p>
  </td>
 </tr>
