  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>An edge cas=
e is
  a special situation at the edge of what your algorithm expects. If your
  function returns the first item in a list, the normal case is a list with=
 at
  least one item. The edge case is an empty list. If your function <span
  class=3DGramE>divides</span> two numbers, the normal case is a nonzero
  denominator. The edge case is division by zero.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:26.4pt;line-height:108%'><span
  style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:108%;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>Safe algori=
thms
  begin by checking dangerous conditions before they cause errors. These ea=
rly
  checks are often called guards or guard clauses because they protect the =
rest
  of the code. A guard might say, &quot;if the list is empty, return
  None,&quot; or &quot;if the denominator is zero, return None.&quot; The p=
oint
  is not to ignore the problem; the point is to choose a safe, predictable
  response instead of letting Python crash with an error.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:0in'><span style=3D'font-size=
:10.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>This matters because real programs rarely
  receive perfect input. Users leave forms blank. Lists are empty. Dictiona=
ries
  are missing keys. Numbers arrive as strings. Files do not exist. APIs res=
pond
  weirdly. Safe algorithms are more reliable because they expect imperfect
  conditions and handle them intentionally.</span></p>
  </td>
