  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:108%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>By Lesson 12, you are expected to combine
  several skills at once. You should be able to look at a code snippet and =
ask:
  Does this run a fixed number of times? Does it loop once per item? Does it
  use a loop inside another loop? Does it double the number of possibilities
  each time? Then you should simplify the answer using Big O rules.</span><=
/p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:26.4pt;line-height:108%'><span
  style=3D'font-size:10.5pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:108%;
  font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>This lesson
  also reinforces that Big O is about growth, not exact timing. A function =
that
  checks only <span class=3DGramE>numbers[</span>0] is <span class=3DGramE>=
O(</span>1),
  because it looks at one item no matter how large the list is. A loop that
  checks every item is O(n), because the work grows with the number of item=
s. A
  nested loop that compares every pair is O(n^2), because the work grows li=
ke n
  times n. A branching process that doubles possibilities can behave like
  O(2^n), which grows extremely fast.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-bottom:0in'><span style=3D'font-size=
:10.5pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial'>The challenge also mixes in safe algorithm
  thinking. Advanced analysis is not only about speed. A good algorithm sho=
uld
  be understandable, should handle edge cases, and should avoid unnecessary
  work. The strongest answer is often the one that is correct, clear, safe,=
 and
  reasonably efficient.</span></p>
