  12.0pt;line-height:105%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial;color:#212121'>An algorithm is not the same thing as code. Code is =
one
  way to express an algorithm, but the algorithm is the thinking underneath.
  This is why the course begins with everyday examples like ordering pizza,
  washing clothes, and making sandwiches. If you can break a real task into
  clear steps, you can eventually turn that same logic into Python.</span><=
/p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:2.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial;
  color:#212121'>Pseudocode is the middle step between natural English and
  Python. It lets you plan the logic without worrying about exact syntax ye=
t.
  Good pseudocode should be clear, ordered, and small enough to translate. =
If
  one line secretly contains five actions, split it. A computer cannot infer
  what you meant the way a person might.</span></p>
