  12.0pt;line-height:105%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
  Arial;color:#212121'>Counting work means identifying the part of the
  algorithm that repeats or grows. You usually do not count every tiny mach=
ine
  operation. Instead, you focus on the key action: printing a name, checkin=
g an
  item, comparing a pair, adding a number, or processing one record.</span>=
</p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
  margin-left:2.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0=
pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial;
  color:#212121'>Big O then turns that count into a growth category. If the=
 key
  work stays the same no matter how much input exists, it is <span class=3D=
GramE>O(</span>1).
  If the work goes one-for-one with the number of items, it is O(n). If each
  item gets compared with many other items, the work usually grows as O(n^2=
).</span></p>
  </td>
