ttom:
  6.4pt;margin-left:2.0pt;line-height:105%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.0pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:105%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;color:#212121'>An algorithm starts as a pla=
n: a
  clear set of steps that transforms a starting situation into a finished
  result. Pseudocode lets you write that plan without worrying about Python
  syntax yet. Once the plan exists, you can count how much work it does. If=
 the
  work is fixed, it is constant. If <span class=3DGramE>the work</span> gro=
ws
  with every item, it is linear. If every item gets paired with every other
  item, it is quadratic.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:.95pt;margin-bo=
ttom:
  6.4pt;margin-left:2.0pt;line-height:105%'><span style=3D'font-size:9.0pt;
  mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;line-height:105%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
  mso-fareast-font-family:Arial;color:#212121'>Big O is the language for na=
ming
  those growth shapes. It does not care about exact seconds or exact tiny
  operations. It cares about how the work grows as the input gets bigger. T=
hat
  is why <span class=3DGramE>constants</span> and smaller terms usually
  disappear. What matters most is the dominant shape.</span></p>
  <p class=3DMsoNormal style=3D'margin-top:0in;margin-right:4.4pt;margin-bo=
ttom:
  0in;margin-left:2.0pt'><span style=3D'font-size:9.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:=
12.0pt;
  line-height:107%;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;mso-fareast-font-family:A=
rial;
  color:#212121'>The final layer is judgment. A better solution is not only=
 one
  that works. It should be clear enough to understand, efficient enough for=
 the
  input size, and safe enough to handle edge cases. That is the Week 3 mind=
set:
  clear steps, counted work, named growth, thoughtful choices, safe behavio=
r.</span></p>
